<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seleucid era</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seleucid calendar era</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Seleucid era was a system of dating years from the recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> in 312/311 BCE. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> profile, Seleucus adopted this event as the foundation date of his empire. The era remained in use across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> for many centuries after the dynasty itself had disappeared.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Seleucid era, also called Anno Graecorum (year of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>) and sometimes the Year of Alexander, was a system of numbering years used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and other Hellenistic civilisations, and later by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>. It dates from the reconquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I Nicator</a> in 312/311 BCE, after his exile in Ptolemaic Egypt, an event that Seleucus and his court considered to mark the founding of the Seleucid Empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Two versions of the Seleucid years existed, differing in when the year began. The natives of the empire used the Babylonian calendar, whose new year falls on 1 Nisanu (3 April in 311 BCE), so that year 1 corresponds roughly to April 311 BCE to March 310 BCE. Coele-Syria followed it, notably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, who call it the Era of Contracts. The Macedonian court adopted the Babylonian calendar with Macedonian month names but reckoned the new year from autumn, on an unknown date, so that year 1 ran from autumn 312 BCE to summer 311 BCE. By the 7th century CE the west Syrian Christians settled on 1 October to 30 September, and Jews reckon each year from the lunar month Tishri.</p><p>Dates between spring and autumn therefore differ by one. The Jewish book 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> generally uses the Babylonian and Judean count, while 2 Maccabees uses the Macedonian version, probably because it was written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Elias Bickerman gave the restoration of the Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> by Judas Maccabaeus, about 15 December 164 BCE, as an example: year 148 by Jewish and Babylonian reckoning, year 149 for the court.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The era remained in use long after the dynasty. It appears in the Zabad inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, dated the 24th of Gorpiaios, 823 (24 September 512 CE), and in the writings of John of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a>. Syriac chroniclers used it until Michael the Syrian in the 12th century CE, and it has been found on Central Asian tombstones of Christians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Church of the East</a> as late as the 14th century.</p><p>Yemenite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> used the count of contracts in their legal deeds and contracts until modern times, a practice derived from an ancient teaching in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talmud">Talmud</a> that required Diaspora Jews to uphold it. It is mentioned in 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> and by the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a>. Most Jewish communities abandoned it after Rabbi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> ben Zimra, as Chief Rabbi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, cancelled the practice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Seleucid era” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seleucus then retook Babylon with a small force, and the event became the starting point of the <b>Seleucid era</b>, a calendar that remained in use across the Near East for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
